\title{TunerAsync using Asynchronous Decentralized Bayesian Optimization on Homogeneous Subspaces}
\description{
\code{TunerADBOSubspaces} class that implements Asynchronous Decentralized Bayesian Optimization (ADBO) on a partition
of the search space into homogeneous subspaces.
Every worker is permanently assigned to one subspace and fits its own \link{SurrogateLearner} on the evaluations of
that subspace only.
This is a minimal interface internally passing on to \link{OptimizerADBOSubspaces}.
For additional information and documentation see \link{OptimizerADBOSubspaces}.

\code{TunerADBOSubspaces} is considered an experimental feature and the API might be subject to changes.
Currently, only single-objective optimization is supported.
}
\section{Subspaces}{

The subspaces are passed as a named list of \link[paradox:ParamSet]{paradox::ParamSet}s via the \code{subspaces} parameter.
Every subspace must be a subset of the search space of the \link[bbotk:OptimInstanceAsyncSingleCrit]{bbotk::OptimInstanceAsyncSingleCrit} and the subspaces
must be disjoint, i.e., every evaluated point must belong to exactly one subspace.
Use \code{\link[=partition_search_space]{partition_search_space()}} to derive such a list from a branching parameter of the search space.

A worker claims a subspace when it starts and keeps it until the optimization terminates.
The number of workers per subspace is set with \code{n_workers_subspace}, e.g., \code{c(gpu = 1L, cpu = 7L)} on a machine
with one GPU and eight CPU cores.
Note that \code{mlr3mbo} only controls which subspace a worker samples from.
Binding a worker to a specific device is the responsibility of the user and can be done with the \code{worker_setup}
function, which is called on the worker after it has claimed its subspace.

Each evaluation is logged into the \link[bbotk:ArchiveAsync]{bbotk::ArchiveAsync} with an additional \code{.subspace} column holding the id of
the subspace it was proposed from.
As for all other columns that are written when an evaluation finishes, \code{.subspace} is \code{NA} for the queued, running,
and failed points that the \link[bbotk:ArchiveAsync]{bbotk::ArchiveAsync} also contains.
}

\section{Initial Design}{

Every subspace receives its own initial design of \code{design_size} points, generated with \code{design_function} on the
subspace.
Use \code{design_size_subspace} to set a different size per subspace, which is usually necessary when the subspaces
differ strongly in dimensionality or in how many evaluations they can afford.
Alternatively, \code{initial_design_subspace} accepts a named list of \code{\link[data.table:data.table]{data.table::data.table()}}s.

In contrast to \link{OptimizerAsyncMbo}, the initial design is not pushed to the shared queue of \CRANpkg{rush},
because any worker could pop any point from it and would thereby leave its subspace.
Instead, the workers of a subspace claim the points of the design of their subspace one by one.
The \code{initial_design} and \code{design_size} parameters of \link{OptimizerAsyncMbo} therefore behave slightly differently and
\code{initial_design} must not be set.
}

